{{nutshell|This page provides guidance on {{strong|when}} to format text in articles. For instructions on {{strong|how}} to do that, see {{section link|Help:Wiki markup|Format}}.}}

{{style}}

This is the part of Wikipedia's [[Wikipedia:Manual of Style|Manual of Style]] which covers when to format text in articles, such as which text should use '''boldface''' or ''italic'' type.

{{TOC limit|3}}

== Boldface ==
{{shortcut|MOS:B|MOS:BOLD}}

[[Boldface]] ('''text like this''') is common in articles, but is considered appropriate only for certain usages. To create it, surround the text to be boldfaced with triple apostrophes (<syntaxhighlight lang="wikitext" inline="">'''Lorem ipsum'''</syntaxhighlight>).{{efn|name=HTML b and i|1=Technically, it is also possible to use the {{tag|b}} HTML element for boldface and the {{tag|i}} element for italics, but that is not recommended style on Wikipedia, except in cases (mostly in template code) where it is technically necessary.}}

=== <span class="anchor" id="TITLE"></span>Article title terms ===
<!-- The above Template:Anchor tag serves to provide a permanent target for incoming section links. Please do not move it out of the section heading. Please do not modify it, even if you modify the section title. -->
{{Main article|Wikipedia:Manual of Style/Lead section#Format of the first sentence}}

Boldface is often applied to the first occurrence of the article's title word or phrase in the [[Wikipedia:Lead|lead]]. This is also done at the first occurrence of a term (commonly a synonym in the lead) that [[WP:Redirect|redirects]] to the article or one of its subsections, whether the term appears in the lead or not {{see below|{{section link||Other uses}} below}}. 

This is not a requirement: for instance, it will not be desirable in a case where a large number of terms redirect to a single article, e.g. a plant species with dozens of vernacular names.

=== <span class="anchor" id="BOLDBOLD"></span>Automatically applied boldface ===
{{shortcut|MOS:BOLDBOLD|MOS:DOUBLEBOLD}}
In the following cases, boldface is applied ''automatically'', either by MediaWiki software or by the browser:

* [[MOS:HEADINGS|Subsection headings]] of level 3 and below (<syntaxhighlight lang="wikitext" inline>===Subheading===</syntaxhighlight>, <syntaxhighlight lang="wikitext" inline>====Sub-subheading====</syntaxhighlight>, etc., markup). There are five heading levels used in writing articles (the top-level one being reserved for the auto-displayed page name).{{efn| name=headings|1=<!--Do we really need to cover all of this as THIS guideline?-->Pages on the [[World Wide Web]] are written in HyperText Markup Language ([[HTML]]); web browsers render HTML as formatted text. The [[MediaWiki]] software that Wikipedia uses converts [[Wikipedia:Wiki markup|wiki markup]] to HTML. HTML has six heading levels, specified in HTML as {{tag|h1}} through {{tag|h6}}. A Wikipedia article or page title is an HTML level-1 heading (and this is not otherwise used on Wikipedia in articles nor, with rare exceptions, in other namespaces). Headings within an article or page use HTML level-2 through -6 headings. At the beginning of a line (only), MediaWiki wiki markup uses the same number of equal signs (<syntaxhighlight lang="wikitext" inline>=</syntaxhighlight>) before and after a heading to determine the heading level. The number of equal signs on either side of a heading corresponds to the HTML heading level: <syntaxhighlight lang="wikitext" inline>==Foo==</syntaxhighlight> is equivalent to <syntaxhighlight lang="wikitext" inline><h2>Foo</h2></syntaxhighlight>. Following best-practices recommendations of [[W3C]] and [[WHATWG]], and the logic of document structuring, Wikipedia does not use a level-3 heading except under a level-2 heading, a level-4 heading except under a level-3 heading, etc.  For practical purposes, it is rare for articles to go below level-4 headings; a perceived need to do so is often an indication that an article is [[Wikipedia:Article size|too long]] and needs to be [[Wikipedia:Split|split]].}}
* Terms in [[MOS:DLIST|description lists]] (example: [[Glossary of the American trucking industry]])
* [[MOS:TABLE|Table headers]] and captions
* A link to the page on which that link appears, called a [[Help:Self link|self link]]

Manually added boldface markup in such cases will end up making excessive double-bold (900 weight) fonts and should be avoided.

=== <span class="anchor" id="OTHER"></span> Other uses ===
<!-- The above Template:Anchor tag serves to provide a permanent target for incoming section links. Please do not move it out of the section heading. Please do not modify it, even if you modify the section title. -->
{{shortcut|MOS:BOLDREDIRECT}}
Use boldface in the remainder of the article only in the following special cases:

* [[Wikipedia:Redirect#What needs to be done on pages that are targets of redirects?|After following a redirect]]: Terms that redirect to an article or section are commonly bolded when they appear in the first couple of paragraphs of the lead section, or at the beginning of another section; for example, subtopics treated in their own sections or alternative names for the main topic. {{crossref|See {{section link||Article title terms}} above.}}
* Mathematical objects that are sometimes written in boldface, such as [[Vector (mathematics and physics)|vectors]] and certain special sets, such as the [[rational number]] symbol '''Q''' {{crossref|(see {{section link|Wikipedia:Manual of Style/Mathematics|Blackboard bold}} for further details)}}
* In some citation formats, for the volume number of a journal or other multi-volume works.

[[WP:Citation template|Citation templates]], such as [[Template:Citation]], automatically supply all formatting (such as italic, boldface, and quotation marks).

{{anchor|strong|Strong|STRONG}}HTML's {{tag|strong|o|link=yes}} emphasis, which usually renders as boldface, can be used in quotations to represent material boldfaced in the original material. The {{tlx|strong|{{var|...}}}} template can be used to easily wrap text in {{tag|strong}} tags.

=== When not to use boldface ===
{{shortcut|MOS:NOBOLD}}
{{Redirect|MOS:NOBOLD|guidelines on when not to bold an article title in the lead|Wikipedia:Manual of Style/Lead section#Avoid boldface}}
Avoid using boldface for emphasis in article text (see {{Section link||Emphasis}} for proper formatting). 
Avoid using boldface for introducing new terms; instead, italics are preferred (see {{Section link||Words as words}}). 

Avoid using boldface (or other font gimmicks) in the expansions of acronyms, as in {{!xt|'''U'''nited '''N'''ations}} {{crossref|(see {{section link|Wikipedia:Manual of Style/Abbreviations|Acronyms}} and {{section link|Wikipedia:Manual of Style/Abbreviations|Expanded forms}} for guidelines on acronym style)}}. The same applies to over-explaining [[portmanteau]] terms; avoid clauses like {{!xt|Texarkana is named for '''Tex'''as and '''Arkan'''s'''a'''s}}.

Although it is possible to put non-[[Latin script|Latin]] alphabets such as [[Greek alphabet|Greek]] or [[Cyrillic script|Cyrillic]] in boldface, this should be avoided.

The {{tag|strong|o}} ({{tlx|strong}}) markup is generally not appropriate in article text<!--Actually, under HTML5, <strong><dfn>...</dfn></strong> is really what we SHOULD be using to boldface the article title and any synonyms/redir-targets in the lead sentence, but we can sort that out later. The important part here is to discourage use of strong all over articles, which does happen.--> except in quoted material (see above), though it is common in project pages, template documentation, talk page discussions, and other non-article contexts.

==<span id="Italic face" ></span>Italic type==
<!-- The above span tag serves to provide a permanent target for incoming section links. Please do not move it out of the section heading, even though it disrupts edit summary generation (you can manually fix the edit summary before saving your changes). Please do not modify it, even if you modify the section title. It is always best to anchor an old section header that has been changed so that links to it won't be broken. -->
{{shortcut|MOS:ITALICS}}
{{further information|Wikipedia:Manual of Style/Titles|Wikipedia:Manual of Style#Italics}}
[[Italic type]] (''text like this'') is produced with double apostrophes around the content to be italicized: <syntaxhighlight lang="wikitext" inline>''...''</syntaxhighlight>.{{efn|name=HTML b and i}} Italics, along with semantic emphasis (usually rendered as italics), are used for various specific purposes in Wikipedia, outlined below.

=== Emphasis ===
{{shortcut|MOS:EMPHASIS}}

The use of italics for emphasis on Wikipedia should follow good English print style. The most [[Wikipedia:Manual of Style/Accessibility|accessible]] way to indicate emphasis is with the HTML {{tag|em|link=y}} element or by enclosing the emphasized text within an {{tlx|em|{{var|...}}}} template. Italics markup (<syntaxhighlight lang="wikitext" inline>''...''</syntaxhighlight>, or {{tag|i|link=y}}) is often used in practice for emphasis, but this use is not [[Semantic HTML|semantically]] correct markup, so emphasis markup is preferred. Italics markup is for non-emphasis purposes, such as for book titles and non-English language phrases, as detailed below.

Emphasis may be used to draw attention to an important word or phrase within a sentence, when the point or thrust of the sentence may otherwise not be apparent to readers, or to stress a contrast:

: {{xt|Gellner accepts that knowledge must be knowledge {{em|of}} something.}}

It may be preferable to avoid the need for emphasis by rewriting a sentence more explicitly. Use of emphasis more than once in a sentence is rarely helpful to readers, unless the emphasized terms are being directly compared (more often a [[#Words as words|words-as-words]] case for regular italics).
See also: {{slink||Quotations}}, below.

Other non-emphasis uses of italics should use <syntaxhighlight lang="wikitext" inline>''...''</syntaxhighlight> markup, {{em|not}} {{tag|em|o}} or {{tnull|em}} markup.{{efn|name=i-not-em|1=In particular, [[words as words]], including introduced [[terms of art]], and non-English words and phrases, use normal typographic italics (<syntaxhighlight lang="wikitext" inline>''...''</syntaxhighlight> or <code><nowiki><i>...</i></nowiki></code> markup, when necessary). Do not use emphasis markup as an "escape" for italic markup. If you have a situation that would result in something like <syntaxhighlight lang="wikitext" inline>''War and Peace'''s plot</syntaxhighlight> (in which the <syntaxhighlight lang="wikitext" inline>''</syntaxhighlight> followed by a possessive apostrophe is apt to be parsed as turning on boldfacing instead of ending the italics), you can rewrite to avoid the possessive, or use a proper escape in various forms, including: <code><nowiki>''War and Peace''&lt;nowiki /&gt;'s plot</nowiki></code>, <code><nowiki><i>War and Peace</i>'s plot</nowiki></code>, or <code><nowiki>''War and Peace''</nowiki>{{tl|'}}<nowiki>s plot</nowiki></code>.}}

Do not use boldfacing for emphasis, as covered in {{slink||When not to use boldface}} above.
Do not use underlining, all caps, or small caps for emphasis, as covered in {{slink||How not to apply emphasis}} below.

===Names and titles===
{{shortcut|MOS:NAT|MOS:NAMESANDTITLES}}
{{Wikipedia:Manual of Style/titles hatnote include|where=MOS:ITALICS#N&T}}

Italics should be used for the following types of names and titles, or abbreviations thereof:

* '''[[Wikipedia:Manual of Style/Titles#Major works|Major works]] of art and artifice,''' such as albums, books, video games, films, musicals, operas, symphonies, paintings, sculptures, newspapers, journals, magazines, epic poems, plays, theses, dissertations, television programs or series, radio shows, comics and comic strips. Medium of publication or presentation is not a factor; a video feature only released on video tape, disc or the Internet is considered a "film" for these purposes, and so on. {{crossref|(See {{section link|WP:Manual of Style/Titles|Italics}} for details.)}}{{pb}}<!--

--> {{em|Minor works}} (and any specifically titled subdivisions of italicized major works) are given in double quotation marks, not italics, even when the title is not in English. {{crossref|selfref=no|(For details, see {{section link||When not to use italics}}.)}}{{pb}}<!--

-->These cases are well-established conventions recognized in most style guides. Do not apply italics to other categories or instances because you feel they are creative or artful (e.g. game or sport moves, logical arguments, "artisanal" products, schools of practice or thought, etc.).
* '''Court case names:''' {{xt|''FCC v. Pacifica''}}. ([[Case citation]] or [[law report]] information is presented in normal font.){{efn|Some legal articles on Wikipedia use [[Bluebook]] legal citation style, which specifies italics for case names in the body of an article (Rule 2.1(a)), and normal (Roman) type for the footnoted citation (Rule 2.2(a)(i)).}}
* '''Legislation''' (acts and laws) in ''some'' countries (e.g. [[Citation of Canadian legislation#Titles|Canada]], [[Wikipedia:Manual of Style/Legal#In Australia|Australia]]): {{xt|''Constitution Act, 1982; Racial Discrimination Act 1975''}}
* '''Certain [[scientific name]]s:'''
** [[Gene]]s (but not [[protein]]s encoded by genes).
** [[Genus|Genera]] (and abbreviation thereof) and all lower [[Rank (zoology)#All ranks|taxa]] (including [[species]] and [[subspecies]]), but not higher taxa (e.g. family, order, etc.). The entire scientific name should be italicized, except where an interpolation is included in or appended to the name. {{crossref|selfref=no|(For details, see {{section link||Scientific names}}.)}}
* {{Anchor|Vessels|VESSELS|Vessel|VESSEL}}'''Named, specific vessels: [[proper names]]''' given to:
** Ships, with [[ship prefixes]], [[Hull classification symbol|classification symbols]], [[pennant number]]s, and types in normal font: {{xt|USS ''Baltimore'' (CA-68)}}. Italicize ship names when they appear in the names of classes of ships ({{xt|the ''Baltimore''-class cruisers}}). {{crossref|(See [[Wikipedia:Naming conventions (ships)]] for more detail on ship and ship class titles.)}}
** Aircraft: {{xt|the ''Spirit of St. Louis''}}
** Spacecraft (including fictional): the {{xt|Space Shuttle ''Challenger''}}, {{xt|''Gaia'' space observatory}}, {{xt|USS ''Enterprise'' NCC-1701}}, {{xt|''Constitution''-class starships}}. Do not italicize a mission, series, or class except where it coincides with a craft's name: the {{xt|''Eagle''}} was the {{xt|Apollo 11 lunar lander}}; {{xt|''Voyager 2''}} was launched as part of the {{xt|Voyager program}}.
** Trains and locomotives: {{xt|the ''City of New Orleans'' (train)}}
: The vessels convention {{em|does not apply}} to smaller conveyances such as cars, trucks, and buses, or to mission names. Also, most real-world spacecraft and rockets at this time are not given proper names, thus {{!xt|''Apollo 11''}}, {{!xt|''Saturn V''}}, {{!xt|''Falcon 9''}}, etc. are not appropriate to apply to spacecraft.

Use [[Wikipedia:Piped link|piped linking]] to properly italicize in wikilinks: "[[USS Baltimore (CA-68)|USS ''Baltimore'' (CA-68)]], the lead ship of the [[Baltimore-class cruiser|''Baltimore''-class cruisers]]", is produced by <syntaxhighlight lang="wikitext" inline>[[USS Baltimore (CA-68)|USS ''Baltimore'' (CA-68)]], the lead ship of the [[Baltimore-class cruiser|''Baltimore''-class cruisers]]</syntaxhighlight>

=== Words as words ===
{{redirect|MOS:WAW|the "writing about women" essay|WP:WAW}}
{{shortcut|MOS:WAW|MOS:WORDSASWORDS}}
Use italics when writing about words as words, or letters as letters (to indicate the [[use–mention distinction]]). Examples:

* {{xt|The term ''panning'' is derived from ''panorama'', which was coined in 1787.}}
* {{xt|''Deuce'' means 'two'.}} ([[Linguistic gloss|Linguistic glosses]] must be [[Wikipedia:Manual of Style#Single quotation marks|nested in single quotation marks]].)
* {{xt|The most common letter in English is ''e''.}}

When italics could cause confusion (such as when italics are already being heavily used in the page for some other purpose, e.g., many non-English words and phrases), double quotation marks may be used instead to distinguish words as words ({{xt|"Just Say No" was an advertising campaign}}). Quotation marks may also be used when a whole sentence is mentioned ({{xt|The preposition in ''She sat on the chair'' is ''on''}}; or {{xt|The preposition in "She sat on the chair" is "on"}}). For very small characters by themselves, code style is helpful: <code>.</code> (this is produced using {{tag|code}}).

{{Anchor|Term|Terms|TERM|TERMS}}
{{shortcut|MOS:TECHNICAL|MOS:TERM}}
A [[Wikipedia:Manual of Style#Technical language|technical or other jargon]] term being introduced is often being mentioned as a word rather than (or in addition to) playing its normal grammatical role; if so, it should be italicized or quoted, usually the former. This first occurrence of the term [[Wikipedia:Manual of Style/Linking#Principles|should also usually be linked]] if it has its own article (or section, or [[Wikipedia:Manual of Style/Glossaries|glossary entry]]) corresponding to the exact meaning of the term as it is used in the article.

Italics may also be used where {{tag|dfn|o}} tags or {{tlx|dfn}} templates mark a term's first use, definition, introduction, or distinguished meaning on the page. As {{tag|dfn|o}} tags and {{tlx|dfn}} templates do not apply text formatting, the italicization (or quoting) must be added if intended, or {{tlx|dfni}} can be used instead. For instance, in the [[Consciousness]] article:

{{ubl|{{in5}}{{xt|''<dfn>Access consciousness</dfn>'' is the phenomenon whereby information in our minds is accessible for verbal report and reasoning.}}
|{{in5}}{{code|''<dfn>Access consciousness</dfn>'' is ...|wikitext}}}}

If, however, a term is an [[MOS:ALTNAME|alternative name]] for the subject of the article (often the target of a [[Wikipedia:Redirect|redirect]]), then [[boldface]] should be used in place of italics or quotation marks at such a first occurrence {{see above|{{slink||Article title terms}}, above}}:

{{in5}}{{xt|The '''small forward''' ('''SF'''), also known as the '''three''', is one of the five positions in a regulation basketball game.}}

{{Anchor|Two styles}}
{{shortcut|MOS:TWOSTYLES}}
Generally, use only one of these styles at a time (do not italicize and quote, or quote and boldface, or italicize and boldface) for words-as-words purposes. Exceptionally, two styles can be combined for distinct purposes such as in the [[MOS:BOLDLEAD|lead sentence bold usage]], e.g. a film title is italicized and it is also boldfaced, or a short work, like a television episode, which is in quotes and is also boldfaced {{crossref|(see [[WP:ITALICTITLE]] for additional requirements and guidance)}}:

* {{xt|'''''Roundhay Garden Scene''''' is a short silent motion picture...}}
* {{xt|"'''A Tale of Two Cities'''" is the third season premiere...}}

Combined styles are also valid in articles about a term or when significant terms redirect to an article, as in:

{{in5}}{{xt|The "'''New World'''" is a term which is applied to...}}

Remain [[MOS:CONSISTENT|consistent]] with a single style in the same material (do not, for example, use italics for words as words in one paragraph, then quotes in another).

=== Non–English language terms<span class="anchor" id="Foreign terms"></span><span class="anchor" id="Non-English terms"></span><span class="anchor" id="Non-English-language terms"></span> ===
{{see also|Wikipedia:Manual of Style#Non-English terms|Wikipedia:Manual of Style/Accessibility#Other languages}}
{{Shortcut|MOS:NONENGITALIC|MOS:FOREIGNITALIC|MOS:FORITA}}
Wikipedia generally uses [[Italic type|italics]] for words and phrases from non-English languages if they are written using the [[Latin script]]. This does not apply to [[loanword]]s or phrases that see everyday use in non-specialized English, such as {{xt|qi}}, {{xt|Gestapo}}, {{xt|samurai}}, {{xt|esprit de corps}}, {{xt|e.g.}}, {{xt|i.e.}}, {{xt|etc.}}—as these have become English-language vocabulary. Use the native spellings of non-English language vocabulary using the Latin alphabet, with or without [[diacritic]]s—otherwise, you should [[Anglicisation (linguistics)|anglicize]] their spelling. For example:
* {{xt|[[Gustav I of Sweden]] liked to breakfast on [[crispbread]] ({{lang|sv|knäckebröd}}) open sandwiches with toppings such as {{lang|sv|messmör}} (butter made from goat's milk), ham, and vegetables.}}
* Code: <syntaxhighlight lang="wikitext" inline>[[Gustav I of Sweden]] liked to breakfast on [[crispbread]] ({{lang|sv|knäckebröd}}) open sandwiches with toppings such as {{lang|sv|messmör}} (butter made from goat's milk), ham, and vegetables.</syntaxhighlight>
The {{tlx|lang}} template and its variants support all [[ISO 639]] language codes, correctly identifying the language and automatically italicizing for you. Please use these templates rather than manually italicizing non-English material. {{Crossref|(See {{section link|WP:Manual of Style/Accessibility#Other languages}} for more information.)}}

Use non-English vocabulary sparingly; for more information, see [[Wikipedia:Writing better articles#Use other languages sparingly|Wikipedia:Writing better articles §&nbsp;Use other languages sparingly]].
Certain specialist or non-English terms are not italicized, including musical terminology like {{xt|minuet}} and {{xt|adagio}}. As a rule of thumb, do not italicize words that appear in multiple major English dictionaries.

{{Anchor|BADITALICS}}{{shortcut|MOS:BADITALICS}}
If it is necessary to include terms written in a non-Latin script, they can be placed in parentheses. {{strong|Text in non-Latin scripts}} (such as [[Greek alphabet|Greek]], [[Cyrillic alphabet|Cyrillic]] or [[Wikipedia:Manual of Style/China-related articles#Characters|Chinese]]) {{strong|should neither be italicized as non-English nor bolded}}, even where this is technically feasible; the difference of script suffices to distinguish it on the page. However, [[Wikipedia:Manual of Style/Titles#Italics|titles of major works that should be italicized]] are italicized in scripts that support that feature (including Latin, Greek and Cyrillic); do not apply italic markup to scripts that do not (including Chinese, Japanese, and Korean).{{efn|name=CJK|1=For indicating titles of works, these three languages surround the title with different kinds of brackets; see {{slink|Chinese punctuation|Punctuation marks}} and {{slink|Japanese punctuation|Quotation marks}}. For emphasis, printed text in Chinese, Japanese, and Korean normally uses a special [[emphasis mark]] placed underneath each character (or Japanese kana or Korean hangul syllable block), but support for this in HTML is poor.}}

Some [[Proper and common nouns|proper name]]s—including personal names, place names, and the names of organizations—are usually not italicized as non-English vocabulary.<ref>On style for organization names, see, for instance: [https://web.archive.org/web/20190608015458/https://www.chicagomanualofstyle.org/search.epl?q=foreign ''Chicago Manual of Style''], at "11.8 Foreign institutions": "If given in the original language, names of foreign&nbsp;... institutions and businesses are presented in roman type and capitalized&nbsp;...". [https://web.archive.org/web/20221031182409/https://sites.google.com/a/ngs.org/ngs-style-manual/home/F/foreign-terms ''National Geographic Style Manual''], at "Foreign terms": "1b. institutions and organizations ... are in roman". [https://www.btb.termiumplus.gc.ca/tpv2guides/guides/wrtps/index-eng.html?lang=eng&lettr=indx_catlog_i&page=9y04Dorq2IUY.html ''Government of Canada – Writing Tips''], at "Italics": "French and foreign words: Do not italicize the names of French or foreign organizations".</ref> However, these may be italicized for other reasons, including when [[#Words as words|the name itself is being referred to]]. For example, non-English names listed as translations in the lead of an article should be italicized, e.g. {{xt|'''Nuremberg''' ({{langx|de|Nürnberg}})}}. Non-English names of works should be italicized just like those in English are, e.g. [[Les Liaisons dangereuses|{{xt|{{lang|fr|Les Liaisons dangereuses}}}}]]. When a name should not be italicized, language markup can still ensure proper pronunciation in screen readers, by using the {{para|italic|unset}} parameter: {{tlx|lang|de|italic{{=}}unset|Nürnberg}}.

For better [[Web accessibility|accessibility]], [[Latin-language]] quotations should not be set in [[all caps]] or [[small caps]]. When reproduced for their content, inscriptions that were originally set in all caps should be transcribed according to standard rules of English capitalization. However, simply undoing caps may result in incorrect orthography; for example, capital ''V'' may represent either the consonant ''v'' or the vowel ''u''.  All-caps or preferably small-caps presentation [[Wikipedia:Manual of Style/Capital letters#All caps|may be preserved]] when it is contextually useful, as in technical [[linguistic]] material and descriptions of [[Artifact (archaeology)|artifacts]].  Editors should be cautious about making [[Wikipedia:No original research|their own interpretations]] when transcribing [[Epigraphy|epigraphic]] and [[numismatic]] sources. Particularly on coins, a character that appears to be a letter may instead be a [[Roman numeral]], a denomination, or a symbol. For articles that reproduce examples of epigraphy or coin legends, editors should consult the orthography of expert secondary sources {{crossref|(see also [[diplomatic transcription]])}}.

{{See also2|documentation for [[Template:Lang]]|[[:Category:Wikipedia multilingual support templates|related templates]] on [[Wikipedia:Accessibility#Other languages|marking up non-English text for accessibility purposes]].}}
